Project HADD — Hybrid Aerodynamic Drone Design

Project HADD (Hybrid Aerodynamic Drone Design) is an experimental initiative aimed at merging fixed-wing efficiency with multi-rotor agility to create a new-generation UAV platform.
It focuses on VTOL capability, AI-driven obstacle avoidance, and energy optimization using reverse charging and adaptive tilt-propeller control.

Core Objectives

Develop a hybrid VTOL-fixed wing drone for extended endurance and precision maneuvering.

Implement AI-based flight correction and obstacle detection using onboard computer vision.

Design a servo-controlled propeller tilt mechanism for seamless transition between hover and cruise.

Optimize power systems through regenerative charging and intelligent battery management.

Current Progress

Servo-based tilt-propeller prototype tested using Arduino UNO.

Reverse-charging and aerodynamic design under validation.

Structural CAD model designed (SolidWorks + Blender).

Initial AI model for obstacle detection trained on test dataset.

Collaborations initiated with UAV and aerospace startups for hybrid design refinement.


 Expected Outcome

A working hybrid UAV prototype demonstrating:

Efficient lift-to-drag performance

AI-assisted autonomous flight

Extended flight time through smart power regeneration

Scalable framework for industrial, agricultural, and surveillance applications

Unique Aspects

Combines aerodynamics + AI + embedded systems.

Built from scratch by a first-year IIIT Una student with early-stage prototypes already tested.

Emphasis on sustainability and efficiency in UAV development.
